# Installed as /etc/default/syslogd. Sourced by /etc/init.d/syslog, which starts
# /usr/sbin/syslogd -- $SYSLOGD. The image ships no copy of this file.
#
# /usr/sbin/syslogd is sysklogd v2.1.1. With no arguments it listens on UDP 514 on
# every interface (measured: netstat -lnu shows 0.0.0.0:514, owned by syslogd), and
# its help text says -a is the sender filter, so with none any host on the LAN can
# write into /home/root/log/messages (inferred, not tested). -s once stops it logging
# remote messages; twice opens no network socket at all, which also gives up
# forwarding — /etc/syslog.conf has no @host line, so there is nothing to forward.
# The /dev/log socket local programs use is unaffected.
SYSLOGD="-s -s"
